      rsiTitle: "RSI",
      rsiBody: "RSI compares the average size of up-moves to the average size of down-moves over the last N bars. That's it. A value of 70 means exactly one thing: the recent rise has been fast relative to recent declines. It doesn't mean «expensive», and it doesn't mean «due for a drop».\n\nThe word «overbought» is misleading here, and that's not a nitpick about terminology: in a strong trend, RSI sits above 70 for weeks, and the whole time it's formally pointing at a reversal that isn't happening. We measured what actually happens after RSI > 70 — below.",
      macdTitle: "MACD",
      macdBody: "MACD is the difference between two exponential moving averages — exactly the object you worked with last chapter. Everything said there about fitted parameters applies here too: 12, 26, and 9 aren't derived constants, they're numbers that stuck historically. A line crossover is a statement that the short average has overtaken the long one. Not a prediction.",
      divTitle: "Divergence",
      divBody: "Divergence is a mismatch between a new price extreme and the absence of one on the oscillator. That's a description of a fact: the momentum of the latest move is weaker than the previous one. What comes next is interpretation, and that's where you need to be careful: the exact same mismatch also shows up in the middle of a strong trend, where nothing reverses at all.\n\nAnd the key part — divergences are mostly visible in hindsight. On a finished chart they're easy to find, because the eye looks for them right next to a reversal that already happened. That's exactly the effect you just measured on yourself.",
